You must edit the `Source` to satisfy the `Edit Requirement`.
Each line in the source is tagged as `LINE:HASH|content`. Use the `LINE:HASH`
prefix as the anchor for any edit you emit.

Emit ONE OR MORE fenced op blocks. Each block is applied in order. Do not
explain, narrate, or add prose outside the blocks.

Op kinds (choose the minimal one that satisfies the requirement):

1. `<<<<SET <LINE:HASH>` / `SET<<<<`
   Replace exactly the line at `<LINE:HASH>` with the body.

   Example:
   <<<<SET 3:7c
       print(msg)
   SET<<<<

2. `<<<<RANGE <start_LINE:HASH> <end_LINE:HASH>` / `RANGE<<<<`
   Replace the inclusive line range from `<start_LINE:HASH>` to
   `<end_LINE:HASH>` with the body. To replace a single line, give the
   same anchor twice.

   Example:
   <<<<RANGE 2:b2 2:b2
       msg = f"Hi, {name}"
   RANGE<<<<

3. `<<<<INSERT_AFTER <LINE:HASH>` / `INSERT_AFTER<<<<`
   Insert the body verbatim as new lines immediately after `<LINE:HASH>`.
   The body must be non-empty.

   Example:
   <<<<INSERT_AFTER 4:91
   greet("everyone")
   INSERT_AFTER<<<<

4. `<<<<REPLACE` / `REPLACE<<<<`  (or `<<<<REPLACE ALL` / `REPLACE<<<<`)
   Fuzzy whitespace-insensitive text substitution. The body is
   `<old_text>\n<new_text>`. Use this only when none of the above
   anchor-based ops can target the change cleanly.

   Example:
   <<<<REPLACE
   foo(  )
   foo(x)
   REPLACE<<<<

